# Subvert - the classics first, and then what this machine has that they did
# not.
#
# Thirteen parameters, and eight of them are one idea: a resonant filter with
# an envelope thrown at it. So the first half of this bank is a tour of that
# one idea - how far open, how much envelope, how fast it shuts, how far a
# note slides into the next - because that is what a bank of this kind is
# judged on, and anyone reaching for this machine is reaching for it first.
#
# The second half is the four controls that are not part of that idea: the
# sub oscillator, the pulse width, the band-pass mode and the drive. None of
# them existed on the instrument this descends from, and they are why the
# machine has its own name rather than being a tribute act.
#
# **Levelled to -21 dB on the harness's loudness column**, which is the house
# figure across every machine in the app rather than a number chosen for this
# bank alone. Getting there took fourteen decibels out of the machine's
# output rather than out of each patch's volume knob (`kHouse` in
# Subvert.cpp): every machine here had been levelled against its own bank and
# none against the others, and the factory had come to span twenty-five
# decibels, so changing machine changed the volume of the song. Done in the
# machine, the knob still means the same thing wherever you are and Init
# lands with the rest of the bank instead of fourteen decibels above it.
#
# Three patches sit below the house figure and all three are band-passed,
# which takes both ends off a spectrum and most of its energy with them:
# Band Lead by three decibels, Ghost by five and Band Squelch by six, all
# three already at the top of their volume. That is what the mode costs and
# not something to pretend away. Drive does not buy it back - its own
# compensation is slightly over-generous, so leaning on it measures quieter.
#
# **The demo is sixteenths with rests, slides and accents** (`role acid`),
# because what this machine does happens between the notes rather than on
# them. The two bars of eighths it used to be showed that a bass machine
# worked and showed nothing about this one.
#
# Every patch says the notes it is played in, so the app puts the keyboard
# there when it is loaded. All 32 notes of every range read within twenty
# cents; Sub Drop stops at 45 because above that the octave below the note is
# only thirteen decibels ahead of it and it is a sub no longer.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Subvert` lists
# them. A patch names only what it changes.

machine Subvert
# Sixteenths with rests, slides and accents, because what this machine is
# for happens between the notes rather than on them. Eighths showed that a
# bass machine worked and showed nothing about this one.
role    acid

patch Init

# --- The classics ----------------------------------------------------
#
# What the machine everybody means does, and what a bank of this kind is
# judged on first.

patch Classic  note=36 range=24..55
  # The one everybody means. Open enough to hear the note, resonant enough to
  # hear the filter, and a decay that shuts before the next eighth.
  #
  # Further from the machine's own defaults than it first was: at 520 Hz and
  # 0.62 it measured the same as Init, and a bank whose first two entries are
  # the same sound has nine patches in it, not ten.
  cutoff         410 Hz
  resonance      0.72
  envmod         0.64
  decay          280 ms
  accent         0.75
  slide          50 ms
  drive          0.16
  volume         0.70

patch Squelch  note=36 range=24..55
  # Further into the resonance and a much faster decay: the blip rather than
  # the sweep. Accent nearly all the way up, because on a line like this the
  # accented steps are the line.
  cutoff         330 Hz
  resonance      0.9
  envmod         0.85
  decay          130 ms
  accent         0.92
  slide          40 ms
  drive          0.22
  volume         0.70

patch Rubber  note=36 range=24..55
  # A pulse instead of a saw, wide slide, and enough drive to round the peak
  # off. The one that bends between notes rather than stepping.
  wave           #1
  pw             0.42
  cutoff         295 Hz
  resonance      0.75
  envmod         0.7
  decay          150 ms
  accent         0.8
  slide          130 ms
  drive          0.25
  volume         0.56

patch "Long Sweep"  note=36 range=24..55
  # A decay far longer than a step, so the filter never closes and the line
  # rises across the bar instead of inside each note.
  cutoff         180 Hz
  resonance      0.72
  envmod         0.95
  decay          1600 ms
  accent         0.45
  slide          60 ms
  drive          0.15
  volume         0.74

patch "Slide Line"  note=36 range=24..55
  # Every note glides into the next. The slide is the other half of what
  # this machine is for and nothing else here is built around it: at half a
  # second the line stops being notes and becomes one moving pitch.
  cutoff         380 Hz
  resonance      0.68
  envmod         0.6
  decay          260 ms
  accent         0.7
  slide          420 ms
  drive          0.14
  volume         0.72

patch Doorstop  note=36 range=24..55
  # Shut. Almost no cutoff, almost all envelope, and a decay short enough
  # that each note is a thud with a pitch somewhere inside it.
  cutoff         90 Hz
  resonance      0.55
  envmod         1
  decay          90 ms
  accent         0.85
  slide          25 ms
  drive          0.3
  volume         0.72

patch Round  note=36 range=24..55
  # No resonance at all and no envelope: the note, warm, with the filter just
  # taking the top off. Every bank needs the one that is simply a bass, and
  # a bank of nothing but acid does not have it.
  cutoff         520 Hz
  resonance      0.0
  envmod         0.0
  decay          300 ms
  accent         0.35
  slide          45 ms
  drive          0.05
  volume         0.92

patch "Open Saw"  note=36 range=24..55
  # The filter out of the way, so you can hear what it is doing to
  # everything else in the bank. Raw oscillator, drive off, and the only
  # thing shaping the note is its own decay.
  cutoff         9000 Hz
  resonance      0.05
  envmod         0.0
  decay          500 ms
  accent         0.3
  drive          0.0
  volume         1.00

# --- Beyond ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# The sub oscillator, the band-pass, the pulse width and the drive are
# four things the machine this descends from never had. This half is
# what they are for, and it is the half the machine is named after.

patch "Sub Hollow"  note=36 range=24..55
  # The sub is the note and the pulse is the edge on it. Narrow width, filter
  # low, very little envelope: a bass that stays where it is put.
  wave           #1
  pw             0.24
  sub            0.65
  cutoff         220 Hz
  resonance      0.38
  envmod         0.3
  decay          480 ms
  accent         0.5
  drive          0.08
  volume         0.69

# Stops at 45: above that the cutoff at the floor leaves the sub with the
# note only thirteen decibels behind it, and it is a sub no longer.
patch "Sub Drop"  note=36 range=24..45
  # All sub and no edge: the cutoff is at the floor, so what is left is the
  # octave below the note and almost nothing else. For the bottom of a mix
  # rather than the front of one.
  wave           #1
  pw             0.5
  sub            1
  cutoff         70 Hz
  resonance      0.2
  envmod         0.1
  decay          900 ms
  accent         0.3
  drive          0.0
  volume         1.00

patch "Band Squelch"  note=36 range=24..55
  # Band-pass: no bottom and no top, so what is left is the resonance itself.
  # Volume at the top and drive doing the rest, and it is still the quietest
  # thing here by two decibels - which is what taking the ends off a spectrum
  # costs, not something to pretend away.
  mode           #1
  cutoff         690 Hz
  resonance      0.85
  envmod         0.8
  decay          120 ms
  accent         0.9
  drive          0.55
  volume         1.00

patch "Band Lead"  note=48 range=36..67
  # Band-pass with the resonance near the top, played an octave up. The
  # machine does leave the bass register, and this is what it sounds like
  # when it does: a thin reedy lead with the filter as its tone.
  mode           #1
  cutoff         1150 Hz
  resonance      0.93
  envmod         0.45
  decay          200 ms
  accent         0.6
  slide          90 ms
  drive          0.35
  volume         1.00

patch "Thin Ice"  note=48 range=36..67
  # Right at the top of the resonance with almost no envelope: it whistles on
  # the note rather than under it. The one that shows resonance is a tone.
  cutoff         1400 Hz
  resonance      0.97
  envmod         0.15
  decay          220 ms
  accent         0.4
  drive          0.05
  volume         0.91

patch Buzzsaw  note=36 range=24..55
  # Filter nearly out of the way and the drive doing the work. Not acid at
  # all - the machine's other end, and the reason drive is in the voice
  # rather than in an insert.
  cutoff         3200 Hz
  resonance      0.25
  envmod         0.25
  decay          400 ms
  accent         0.55
  drive          0.85
  volume         1.00

patch "Hollow Pulse"  note=36 range=24..55
  # The pulse squeezed almost shut. A narrow pulse is all odd harmonics and
  # no body, which the filter then has almost nothing to take away from: it
  # is nasal where everything above it is round, and it is a width the
  # machine this descends from could not set.
  wave           #1
  pw             0.08
  cutoff         700 Hz
  resonance      0.55
  envmod         0.5
  decay          220 ms
  accent         0.6
  slide          55 ms
  drive          0.12
  volume         0.55

patch Screamer  note=36 range=24..55
  # Drive at the top with the resonance still up, which the original could
  # only do by putting a pedal after it. The filter is still doing its job
  # underneath; what comes out the other side is not clean and is not meant
  # to be.
  cutoff         620 Hz
  resonance      0.8
  envmod         0.75
  decay          240 ms
  accent         0.8
  slide          45 ms
  drive          1
  volume         1

patch Ghost  note=48 range=36..67
  # Band-pass with the resonance low and the decay long: no bottom, no top
  # and no bite, so what is left is a breathy middle that sits under a track
  # rather than on it. The machine as a texture rather than a line.
  mode           #1
  # The band sits on the note rather than three octaves above it: at 900 Hz
  # a bass note's fundamental is nowhere near the pass band and the patch was
  # ten decibels under the bank instead of five.
  cutoff         380 Hz
  resonance      0.5
  envmod         0.25
  decay          1400 ms
  accent         0.3
  slide          180 ms
  drive          0.1
  volume         1
